<h1>About</h1>
<p class="byline">By Eshan Pradhan</p>
<p>
  I'm a high school student writing about algorithmic bias and clinical data
  ethics. This site is where I work through what I'm reading and thinking about
  in public, one essay at a time.
</p>
<p>
  The topic pulled me in because health algorithms make consequential decisions
  quietly. A risk score that decides who gets extra care, a triage model trained
  on records that never represented everyone equally &mdash; these systems inherit
  the gaps in the data they learn from, and the people affected rarely get to see
  the math. I want to understand how that happens, and write about it in a way
  that doesn't require a research background to follow.
</p>
<p>
  Expect a new essay roughly once a month. Some will be technical &mdash; how a
  model is built, what a dataset actually measures, where a metric misleads.
  Others will lean toward policy: consent, regulation, and who is accountable
  when an algorithm gets it wrong. I try to show my work and flag what I'm still
  unsure about.
</p>
